.TH FONDE 1 "2026-07-31" "bibliothecaire" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
fonde \- admit material into the library only on a citation that checks out
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fonde
[\fBcorpus\fR]
.br
.B fonde corpus
[\fB\-\-briefs\fR] [\fB\-\-sources\fR] [\fB\-\-coverage\fR] [\fB\-\-export\fR]
.br
.B fonde corpus
[\fB\-\-require\-briefs\fR] [\fB\-\-require\-sources\fR] [\fB\-\-require\-coverage\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B fonde consign
\fIpath\fR...
.br
.B fonde
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-\-summon\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fonde
is the founding act of
.BR bibliothecaire .
To found a thing is to lay down what the rest stands on, so
.B fonde
admits material into the library on one condition: a later reader can check
the citation without asking anyone. Material that fails that condition stays
out. Nothing else about the material \- its interest, its provenance, its
author \- is
.BR fonde 's
business.
.PP
The corpus is the quote record. Each entry carries an id, a text, an author,
a work, a year, a theme and a status.
.B fonde
rejects an entry whose id is not a lowercase kebab\-case slug, whose id or
text repeats one already admitted, whose year is not an integer, whose theme
or status is outside the declared vocabularies, or which claims a publishable
status without a locator and a
.B verified_via
naming the source actually consulted. An entry marked
.B verified\-secondary
states in
.B quoted_in
the author, work and year of the secondary source, carrying a page or section
number into it: a secondary citation with no locator is an assertion rather
than a reference, and
.B fonde
treats it as one.
.PP
A duplicate id is rejected for a reason worth stating, because it looks
harmless. Briefs cite the corpus by id. Two entries sharing an id make every
citation of that id ambiguous, which is a broken reference in every brief that
already made it.
.PP
The library's own prose is held to the same condition. A document that leaves
a repository during a bashify pass and lands nowhere retrievable has failed it
exactly as a quote with no locator does, so
.B fonde
also deposits prose into the vault, where being linked is what makes it
findable again. It copies; it removes nothing. Deleting the original is a
separate act, taken by a caller who has read what
.B consign
reported.
.TP
.B corpus
Read the quote record and report what is admissible. This is the default: run
with no subcommand,
.B fonde
does exactly this.
.TP
.B consign
Deposit each named path into the vault as linked data, carrying the provenance
a later reader needs to follow it back: the repository it came from, the path
inside it, and the commit it stood at. The original is left where it is, and
what is now safe to remove is reported. A vault that is not under version
control is refused rather than written to: a deposit there stands as the only
copy of the document, with no history behind it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-briefs
Report which admitted quotes no brief cites, and which ids the briefs cite
that the corpus does not hold.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-briefs
As
.BR \-\-briefs ,
except a discrepancy is a rejection rather than a report.
.TP
.B \-\-sources
Report the held source material against the ledger: files on disk that no
entry uses, files the ledger does not account for, and downloads short enough
to be stubs of themselves.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-sources
As
.BR \-\-sources ,
except a discrepancy is a rejection rather than a report.
.TP
.B \-\-coverage
Report, per theme, how many admitted quotes stand against the declared
milestone bar, marking each theme that falls short of it.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-coverage
As
.BR \-\-coverage ,
except a theme below the bar is a rejection rather than a report.
.TP
.B \-\-export
Write the admitted quotes to the plain\-text corpus file, one per line, in the
order the record holds them.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Report the same findings as a JSON object on standard output.
.TP
.B \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Report nothing on standard output; the exit status carries the verdict.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Report this utility's own surface: its subcommands, its flags, and which of
them can spend.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Report the version of this utility.
.TP
.B \-\-summon
Permit this run to spend money on work that has no mechanism behind it yet.
Only
.B consign
can spend. Passed with no subcommand, no work is requested and
.B fonde
exits 2 rather than spending or silently running
.BR corpus .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything asked for was checked, and it held.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: an unknown subcommand, an unknown flag, or a flag the named subcommand
does not take.
.TP
.B 3
A summon is needed for what was asked and
.B \-\-summon
was not given.
.TP
.B 4
No mechanism and no contract to summon one:
.B basheur
is not reachable, or holds no contract for the work asked of it.
.TP
.B 5
The quote record is missing, is not valid JSON, or is not a non\-empty array.
The library cannot be founded on it.
.TP
.B 6
The corpus, the briefs or the held sources cannot be read. Reported as
blindness, never as a clean run over what happened to be readable.
.TP
.B 7
The material was read and refused: an entry, a brief citation, a source, or a
theme failed the condition this utility exists to hold, or the vault named for
a deposit is not under version control.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask what the corpus admits today:
.PP
.nf
$ fonde
validate-quotes: OK -- 54 quotes, 50 publishable (47 primary, 3 secondary), 4 seed-unverified
$ echo $?
0
.fi
.PP
Ask where the themes stand against the milestone bar:
.PP
.nf
$ fonde corpus --coverage
validate-quotes: OK -- 54 quotes, 50 publishable (47 primary, 3 secondary), 4 seed-unverified
validate-quotes: coverage (milestone bar: >=2 verified/theme)
  ok    closed-loops         3
  ok    holons               2
  ok    requisite-variety    8
  ok    stigmergy            3
  ok    theory-of-the-firm   3
  SHORT vsm                  1
  ok    wu-wei               3
validate-quotes: 1 theme(s) below the milestone bar: vsm
.fi
.SH COST
.B corpus
never spends. It reads files on this machine and contacts nothing.
.PP
.B consign
can spend, and is the only subcommand that can. As of 2026-08-01 it
.B costs nothing:
the contract behind it,
.BR consign\-prose ,
is mechanized, and this promise has already been kept once. A summon performed
the first deposit
.I and
left behind the implementation that performs every later one unaided, so the
cost of this subcommand fell to zero at its first use and stays there.
.PP
So an ordinary
.B fonde consign
now deposits, spends nothing, and needs no flag. What
.B \-\-summon
grants is permission to spend
.I where no mechanism exists;
it never instructs a run to spend, and it is not a precondition for work that
is already free.
.PP
Which of those two cases holds is a question for
.BR basheur ,
and it is asked on every run rather than assumed here: a run consults the
contract store first, performs the deposit if the contract is mechanized, and
only then \(em on being told an agent is genuinely required \(em withholds the
summon and reports what it would have cost. This verb once answered that
question itself, announcing that no mechanism existed while one did, which
gated free work behind a cost flag and left every repository holding
unconsigned prose unremovable by
.BR fauche (1).
.PP
.B \-\-summon
alone, with no subcommand, requests no work and is a usage error rather than a
silent run of
.BR corpus .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR fsck (8),
.BR jq (1),
.BR shellcheck (1)
